== 2. Gross-Zagier: primes dividing j(tau_d) are non-split in Q(sqrt(-d)) and in Q(sqrt(-3))
   d=  1: |j| = 2^6 * 3^3                    all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 3 <= 3|D|/4 = 3.0
   d=  2: |j| = 2^6 * 5^3                    all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 5 <= 3|D|/4 = 6.0
   d=3: j = 0 (tau is the cube root of unity point)
   d=  7: |j| = 3^3 * 5^3                    all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 5 <= 3|D|/4 = 5.25
   d= 11: |j| = 2^15                         all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 2 <= 3|D|/4 = 8.25
   d= 19: |j| = 2^15 * 3^3                   all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 3 <= 3|D|/4 = 14.25
   d= 43: |j| = 2^18 * 3^3 * 5^3             all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 5 <= 3|D|/4 = 32.25
   d= 67: |j| = 2^15 * 3^3 * 5^3 * 11^3      all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 11 <= 3|D|/4 = 50.25
   d=163: |j| = 2^18 * 3^3 * 5^3 * 23^3 * 29^3 all non-split in both fields: True;  largest prime 29 <= 3|D|/4 = 122.25
   d = 7: the only primes <= 21/4 that are non-split in Q(sqrt(-7)) are 3 and 5 (2 splits), and j(tau_7) = -3^3 5^3.

== 3. Q(sqrt(-7)) as the decomposition field of 2 for the trivial cycle
   doubling orbit of 1 mod 7: [1, 2, 4]  = quadratic residues mod 7: [1, 2, 4]   ord_7(2) = 3 = (7 - 1)/2
   Gauss period g = z + z^2 + z^4 = -0.500000000000+1.322875655532j;  (-1 + sqrt(-7))/2 = -0.500000000000+1.322875655532j;  |g^2 + g + 2| = 6.66e-16;  g*conj(g) = 2.000000000000
   Im g = sqrt(7)/2 > 0: True ;  g + 1 = tau_7 = (1 + sqrt(-7))/2: True ;  j(g) = (-3375.0 - 1.966693969767821675092666e-57j) True
   Paley P7 = Cay(Z7,{1,2,4}) eigenvalues: [(-0.5-1.322875656j), (-0.5-1.322875656j), (-0.5-1.322875656j), (-0.5+1.322875656j), (-0.5+1.322875656j), (-0.5+1.322875656j), (3+0j)]  = 3, g (x3), conj g (x3)
   trace Tr_{F8/F2}(a^i) for a^3+a+1=0: {1: 0, 2: 0, 3: 1, 4: 0, 5: 1, 6: 1}  -> trace-zero exponents [1, 2, 4]
   2-adic root of x^2+x+2: g = 763670323290 mod 2^40, v_2(g) = 1;  (1 + 2g)^2 = -7 mod 2^40: True;  Frobenius 1 + g = -conj(g) is a 2-adic unit
   2-adic root of x^2+x+2: g = 335841304485 mod 2^40, v_2(g) = 0;  (1 + 2g)^2 = -7 mod 2^40: True;  Frobenius 1 + g = -conj(g) is a 2-adic non-unit
   Phi(1) = sum parity_i 2^i = -1/7 mod 2^40: True ;  (1 + 2g)^2 Phi(1) = 1 mod 2^40 for both roots g: True
   real numerators {1,2,4} and 2-adic numerators -{1,2,4} = {3,5,6} are the two cosets of <2> in (Z/7)^*, i.e. the two primes above 2; complex conjugation x -> -x swaps them: [3, 5, 6]

== 4. Visibility: which Heegner fields lie inside a decomposition field of 2?
   Q(sqrt D) lies in Q(zeta_m)^<2> (m odd) iff |D| divides m and chi_D(2) = +1 (2 splits).
   D =    -4: chi_D(2) = +0;  odd conductor: False;  visible to binary codes: False
   D =    -8: chi_D(2) = +0;  odd conductor: False;  visible to binary codes: False
   D =    -3: chi_D(2) = -1;  odd conductor: True;  visible to binary codes: False
   D =    -7: chi_D(2) = +1;  odd conductor: True;  visible to binary codes: True
   D =   -11: chi_D(2) = -1;  odd conductor: True;  visible to binary codes: False
   D =   -19: chi_D(2) = -1;  odd conductor: True;  visible to binary codes: False
